Terraform + CDKTF: Deploying AWS Infrastructure Using a Familiar Programming Language
I recently tried deploying an AWS S3 bucket using Python + CDKTF instead of writing Terraform HCL.

The plan was simple:
• Create an S3 bucket
• Enable versioning
• Add encryption
• Block public access
• Add proper tags
Everything looked straightforward, but I ran into a problem during the setup.

The Problem
I first tried using my normal/system Python environment.
It didn’t work as expected.

How I Debugged It
Rather than changing my system Python setup, I created a Python virtual environment (venv) and installed the required CDKTF packages there.

System Python → Didn’t work
Python venv → Worked
Once that was set up, I tested the Python code and ran:
cdktf synth
Then: cdktf diff

This helped me check what Terraform was going to create before actually deploying it.

The Solution
Finally, I ran:
cdktf deploy
And this time, it worked.
The S3 bucket was created successfully with versioning, encryption, public access blocked, and the required tags.

The biggest takeaway for me was pretty simple
When working with Python-based infrastructure tools, using a separate virtual environment can save a lot of time and helps avoid messing with the system Python setup.

Have you faced a similar problem?

Have you tried using Python + CDKTF for AWS infrastructure?

How did you fix it?
